## Response shape

- All API responses must be JSON objects with at least:
  - `success`: boolean
  - `message`: string
  - Additional data fields as needed, for example:
    - `blogs`, `blog`
    - `comments`, `comment`
    - `dashboard`
- Error responses (including validation errors, 401/403, and 404) must also follow this shape with `success: false`.

## Server responsibilities

- Controllers and middleware should:
  - Return appropriate HTTP status codes (for example `200`, `201`, `400`, `401`, `403`, `404`, `500`).
  - Include clear, user-facing `message` strings that the client can show directly.
  - Use consistent data key names that match client expectations (`blogs`, `comments`, etc.).
- When changing the response structure of an endpoint, update:
  - The corresponding controller or middleware.
  - Any fixtures or seed data that rely on that shape.

## Client responsibilities

- Client API modules in `client/src/api` and hooks in `client/src/hooks/api` should:
  - Always read `response.data.success` and `response.data.message`.
  - Treat any `success: false` as an error, show the message via `react-hot-toast`, and set error state.
  - Safely access optional fields using null checks (for example `data?.blogs || []`).
- When an endpoint’s response shape changes, update:
  - The API method in the appropriate `*Api` module.
  - The related query or mutation hook.
  - Components only as needed, keeping data-shape logic inside hooks when possible.

## Architecture overview

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  userBrowser[UserBrowser] --> clientReact[ClientReact]
  clientReact --> hooksLayer[HooksLayer]
  hooksLayer --> apiLayer[ApiLayer]
  apiLayer --> expressServer[ExpressServer]
  expressServer --> controllers[Controllers]
  controllers --> middlewareChain[MiddlewareChain]
  middlewareChain --> mongoDb[MongoDB]
```

